## Why This Is Asked

This question probes understanding of idempotency, distributed transactions, and fault tolerance in real-time payment systems. It mirrors fintech patterns at Plaid and Lyft, where user actions must not duplicate or lose funds.

## Key Concepts

- Idempotency keys
- Outbox pattern and sagas
- Exactly-once vs at-least-once
- Dead-letter queues and backoff strategies

## Code Example

```javascript
// Sketch: idempotency check and outbox write
function process(tx, store) {
  const key = tx.id;
  if (store.exists(key)) return;
  store.saveOutbox(tx);
  // publish events ...
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test idempotency guarantees under partial failures?
- How do you monitor for orphaned or duplicate transactions in production?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests distributed-systems thinking: exactly-once, dedup, backpressure, observability.

## Key Concepts

- Exactly-once processing
- Deduplication with dedup_id
- Backpressure strategies (batching, pause vs drop)
- Observability (tracing, metrics, logs)
- Kafka vs Kinesis trade-offs
- Idempotent sinks and compensating actions

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudocode for idempotent sink write
function writeEvent(evt, sink, store) {
  const id = evt.dedup_id;
  if (store.seen(id)) return;
  sink.write(evt);
  store.markSeen(id);
}
```

